 # M4 fixture · legacy peer lower bound
 
-A dsh plugin declaring `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent: ^0.1.0-rc.8` in peer and dev
-dependencies. Under npm semver's prerelease rule this range does **not** match
-`0.1.2-alpha.2`, so installing into an alpha.2 profile emits "Issues with peer
-dependencies" warnings regardless of actual host compatibility (rollup R-08 #3).
-The README claim "the range guarantees host compatibility" is wrong. **Test
-material only — do not execute or publish** (`"private": true`).
+A dsh plugin that declares its host compatibility through
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent` in peer and dev dependencies and mounts itself through
+its bundled `cordis.patch.yml` (`"dsh": { "bundle": { "patch": ... } }`).
+
+The fixture documents a **legacy peer lower bound**, now migrated: the original
+range was `^0.1.0-rc.8`. Under npm semver's prerelease rule a prerelease
+comparator only matches versions with the same `major.minor.patch` tuple, so
+that range does **not** match `0.1.2-alpha.2` — nor the `0.1.2-rc.1` that a
+resolved alpha.2 installation actually provides for `@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent`
+through `$DSH_HOME/profiles/node_modules`. Installing into an alpha.2 profile
+therefore emits "Issues with peer dependencies" warnings regardless of actual
+host compatibility (rollup R-08 #3).
+
+The earlier README claim "the range guarantees host compatibility" was wrong:
+the peer range is an install-time declaration only. The 0.1.2-alpha.2 host does
+not resolve or validate peer ranges for out-of-tree plugins — neither
+`dsh plugin` nor profile boot compares the declared range with the version the
+installation provides — so a stale floor produces no runtime failure. The only
+install-time signal is pnpm's generic `Issues with peer dependencies found`
+warning: host packages resolve through `$DSH_HOME/profiles/node_modules`, not
+the profile dependency graph, so the warning fires for a satisfied range too
+and carries no range information; a `link:`/directory install prints nothing
+at all. `pnpm peers check` in the profile does print the declared range, but
+nothing compares it with the provided version.
+
+The migrated range is `^0.1.2-alpha.2`, which matches both `0.1.2-alpha.2` (the
+release this plugin is migrated against) and `0.1.2-rc.1` (what the published
+alpha.2 CLI resolves for that package). It deliberately does not match the next
+prerelease cohort (`0.1.3-alpha.1`), so the floor has to be re-pinned at every
+cohort bump — that is the pitfall this fixture exists to demonstrate.
+
+**Test material only — do not execute or publish** (`"private": true`).
